ave had to be  canceled in hard hit fort mcmurray  northern alberta indigenous groups say  more restrictions are needed to protect  the health system from being overwhelmed  it's a fiasco it's a mess it's uh you  know it's astonishing that  the alberta government with everything  that they have before them  they dropped the ball on fort mcmurray  wood buffalo region the province says it  will send more vaccine to the area  for the next two weeks doses of the  johnson and johnson vaccine will head to  the hard-hit regions  of wood buffalo and banff adults as  young as 30 will become eligible for the  astrazeneca vaccine here too  all in an effort to protect more  essential workers  according to alberta health there are  active outbreaks at 12 oil sands work  sites accounting for more than  700 active cases with 40 percent of  infections reported at the horizon site  operated by canadian natural resources  angela johansen's dad works at horizon  and has been sick for the last nine days  since the 19th he's been in an isolation  room  at the camp at port mcmurray they told  him there's 200 people they got to take  care of so they can't  give him any special requests because he  asked for some suit  just one of more than 20 000 albertans  who are ill  as the province battles its third wave  heather urex west global news  calgary the first doses of the johnson  and johnson vaccine have now arrived in  canada roughly 300 000 doses landed this  afternoon at toronto's pearson airport  the doses arrived frozen and they must  be thawed before they're shipped to the  provinces  next week the alberta government plans  to send its share of those doses to  specific  areas premier jason kenney says  shipments expected in the next two weeks  will go  to two hard-hit communities banff and  the municipality of wood buffalo  and everyone aged 30 and up will be able  to get them  accelerating the vaccination program in  those communities which also happen to  be have younger populations  would be helpful in addressing  the outbreaks  in ontario after months of pressure from  the public and its own science  and medical experts the ontario  government has unveiled a plan for a  paid sick leave program  it's not in place yet it needs  legislation to be passed but if that  happens it will allow for three paid 60  days without the need for a sick note  global's queen's park bureau chief  travis dan raj is with me travis start  with what's in this plan  so this program is retroactive and goes  from april 19th to september 25th  it will cover three paid sick days for  part-time and full-time workers  the program will be administered by the  wsib that is the workplace safety  insurance board  payments will be up to two hundred  dollars a day and a worker will be  eligible if they're sick  they have symptoms they need to get  vaccinated or are waiting in isolation  for results here's what the minister of  labor said for ontario monty mcnaughton  and also andrea horvath the leader of  ontario's ndp  well look we're leading canada when it  comes to  protecting workers we're bringing in  protections for three paid sick days  the federal program runs for four weeks  we're there for workers through this  entire pandemic  i mean three days of of paid leave is  paid sick leave is  it's better than none but i don't know  where the premier thinks  that this is the best paid sick days  program  in north america it certainly is not  travis ontario wanted to piggyback on  the federal sick leave program and it's  now talking to the feds about that  correct that is correct they wanted to  top up the federal program by 500  additional dollars for a total of one  thousand dollars a week the fed said  that they wanted to see a provincial  program  first now that has happened so the ball  right now in ottawa's court donna  all right travis danraj in toronto  thanks in ontario a provincial emergency  order has been amended  allowing hospitals to transfer  chronically ill patients from hospitals  into  long-term care homes without their  consent to free up  hospital beds this temporary emergency  order will provide  hospitals with the flexibility to  transfer certain patients  those who've doctors have determined no  longer require hospital care  and could be better supported in a more  appropriate setting  to a long-term care or retirement home  this would be done in only the most  urgent of situations  ontario's health minister christine  elliott says hundreds of patients in  hospital are waiting to be discharged  to a long-term care home she says  patients or their families will be  consulted but there may be rare  situations where a patient is moved  without consent the order is being  criticized there are fears people will  be railroaded into homes they don't want  to be in  plus care homes have serious staff  shortages  one bioethicist though says this  i do think we have no choice and i do  think it's justified  i i just hope and believe that the  people will move heaven and earth to try  and get consent  uh from all the many people involved  with this and i believe they will  we know the terrible consequences of the  pandemic on residents in long-term care  in canada  today ontario's auditor general made the  failures clear by the time covet 19  started to ravage care homes last march  she says the system was not prepared or  equipped and it was obvious  aggressive infection prevention  detection and patient care actions were  needed and needed quickly  to prevent staggering death rates that  didn't happen  nearly 3 800 residents and 11 staff  members in ontario's long-term care  system  have died since the pandemic began and  the second wave hit ontario care homes  harder than the first eric sorenson  looks at the multiple failures in a  sector  that has long been neglected long-term  care residents rushed into ambulances  couldn't fully convey the horror  inside long-term care homes overcrowded  rooms  insufficient staffing and a virus  running rampant  the auditor general says neither the  government nor the long-term care sector  itself was sufficiently prepared for the  pandemic  or equipped to respond in fact some  patients with covet 19  at first were transferred into long-term  care homes i would say there could have  been more clarity and guidance the  safety of residents in long-term care  homes needs to be considered the  government acknowledges long-term care  should have played a bigger role in the  initial health care response  we have to have a much better integrated  system  where long-term care is seen to be  part of the healthcare sector  as a whole and there were unintended  consequences families were kept from  visiting to curb the spread of the virus  but says the report it had the effect of  further limiting care providers  maureen mcdermott could only wave to her  ailing mother inside  families and that connection that's  absolutely necessary and  the worst part for a child i think is to  have their parents suffer in any way  the report questioned a government  decision to discontinue comprehensive  inspections of long-term care homes to  deal with a years-long backlog of  complaints  on most findings however the minister  blamed the previous liberal government  i'm here to try and fix long-term care  but if the building's on fire and you're  running into it to to try and save  people  yeah i think that's pretty much what we  tried to do but we didn't start the fire  advocates say the current government  must accept its share of responsibility  we knew that long-term care was going to  be most susceptible  and this government stubbornly refused  to take care of our most vulnerable  the auditor general says there has to be  greater accountability and it can be  demonstrated  by ensuring seniors will be cared for  appropriately in the future  eric sorenson global news toronto  in nova scotia it is day one of a  two-week province-wide  lockdown daily new cases of covid 19  reached record levels there this week  there was a slight drop today though 75  new cases compared to 96 yesterday  i too am pleased to see that our case  numbers have gone down but it is only  one day  it's quite possible they may go back up  again tomorrow  what we want and what we're looking for  is a sustained  downward trend nova scotia is now  opening up appointments for the  astrazeneca oxford  vac scene to people aged 40 to 54.  that'll happen later this week  26 percent of nova scotians have had at  least one vaccine dose now the national  average is about  31 percent for months nova scotia had so  few cases it was the envy of much of the  country  but as jesse thomas explains what's  happening now as a reminder  how quickly things can change three  weeks ago the focus in nova scotia was  on the potential re-emergence of the  atlantic bubble  that would allow residents of new  brunswick pei nova scotia  and newfoundland and labrador to travel  freely between provinces again  without having to quarantine but on  tuesday nova scotia announced it was  going into a full  province-wide lockdown how did things  change so fast  variant virus got into the province  that's number one i think we found it  fairly quickly  because we were doing a fair amount of  testing nova scotia had been the envy of  the rest of canada with minimal cases  and few restrictions the province opened  its borders to the rest of atlantic  canada on april 7th  and at the same time allowed retail  establishments and restaurants to return  to 100  capacity because we were at an enviable  place  of having fewer restrictions it was able  to spread  and to go into community fairly quickly  on day one of the lockdown it was  noticeably quiet in downtown halifax  most retail businesses have been forced  to close only essential businesses like  grocery stores can remain open at 25  capacity the doors have closed at dear  friend a cocktail and wine bar that  opened  last summer owner matt boyle has had to  lay off his staff a third time  his new business doesn't qualify for  many of the government benefits  if you don't have comparables you can't  show reduction  so we don't qualify for the wage subsidy  we don't qualify for a lot of the  emergency benefits out there  nova scotia tested more than 10 thousand  residents on tuesday that's been one of  the province's best strategies in  containing the virus says barrett  we want to keep our testing higher than  anywhere else in canada  per capita at the beginning of the  outbreak before it gets out of control  74 members of the canadian armed forces  have been deployed across the province  to offer support at testing sites  this week alone 168 nova scotia health  employees have had to self-isolate as  per covet 19 protocols  jesse thomas global news halifax global  news has uncovered exclusive new details  about a decision  back in 2015 to name general jonathan  vance as canada's top  soldier he was appointed chief of the  defense staff by the conservative  government of stephen harper  and when vance was sworn in an  investigation was underway into his  conduct overseas now new documents  reveal that within hours of vance taking  command  military police recommended ending that  investigation  michael couture explains  at the same time as general jonathan  vance was being sworn in as the chief of  the defense staff in 2015  a military police investigation of him  was still  technically open that's according to  documents obtained by global news  through access to information laws the  investigation was looking into vance's  conduct while serving as deputy  commander  of nato allied joint force command in  naples italy  a position he held from 2013 until july  2014. the 455  heavily redacted document shows two  brief references  to concluding the investigation global  news has confirmed with the department  of national defense the recommendation  to end the investigation  came on july 17th the same day  vance was being sworn in as the  country's top soldier  four days later military police approved  the end of the investigation  there had been no reprimand of the  general a former chief of staff to then  prime minister stephen harper ray novak  testified before a comments committee  last month  about vance's appointment we were  briefed that the email  alleged an inappropriate relationship  during the general's time at nato  but contained no new information novak  testified that it still triggered a  review  by the military police however a week  later harper's national security adviser  told the prime minister no further  information regarding the allegation was  found  [Applause]  there remain questions about the chain  of command and the military police's  ability to investigate  sexual misconduct cases let alone one  involving canada's former top soldier  one of the women at the heart of the  allegations against vance says the  former cds told her  he was above investigation the cds told  me  he was untouchable he owned the cfnis  all of this comes as prime minister  justin trudeau claims no one in his  office knew allegations about vance were  a quote  metoo complaint opposition parties are  pushing to have his chief of staff katie  telford  testify before a commons committee  michael couter global news ottawa  india's deepening disaster coming up  sickness and death is everywhere as  countries  rush to help  in india covet 19 is still spreading  unchecked in just 24 hours another  record  more than 360 000 new infections  reported  and more than 3 000 reported deaths the  real number is believed to be  even higher india's official death toll  is now more than 200 thousand  crystal gomansi reports  his last visit to canada was a few years  ago  sudia khan patel's nephew thought he was  going to be  okay but babu as he called him didn't  make it  so he was you know somebody who always  used to watch zap us jokes and things  like that  so he was very active still you know  still a big part of our lives despite  being far away the struggles in  india are profound and heartbreaking  people are lining up for hours and  paying  random inflated prices for oxygen  desperate to save their loved ones and  death  doesn't end the nightmare crematoriums  around the country  can't keep up i have right now  14 relatives in india that are actively  infected with colvin  some of those are in health care and so  i'm getting first-hand accounts and  and everything you see on tv it's you  know it even that  doesn't relate how desperate the  situation is in the big cities  there are three known virus variants  circulating  but testing and sequencing are  inadequate  so the extent of the virus in india is  unknown  i feel like i'm running a clinic on  whatsapp honestly with all these  different chat groups and  what's your latest saturation they're  sending me their ct scan on whatsapp  two doctors who know the virus and feel  helpless one of the troubling things for  me is that he did die  alone without any family there which you  know has an icu doctor i've seen  unfortunately many times and it's  definitely been the hardest part of this  a gut-wrenching experience for far too  many  crystal gamson global news london  ahead what members of the canadian  military police wrote online about a  woman accusing a top soldier of  misconduct  there's a disturbing new development  tonight involving the sexual misconduct  allegations within the canadian military  a flight of memes and comments in a  private facebook group disparage one of  the women at the heart of the  accusations  the group includes both past and present  members of the canadian military  police the very team investigating two  defense chiefs including  general jonathan vance vance has denied  all allegations of inappropriate  behavior  including a claim from kelly brennan  that he fathered  two of her children abigail beeman has  the story and a warning there is  some bad language in it  disciplined by example canada's military  police motto  but facing two of its most sensitive  investigations yet  the question is what kind of example a  string of comments in a private facebook  group for current and former officers  mocks testimony by major kelly brennan  one of the women alleging sexual  misconduct by former defense chief  general jonathan vance if you sleep with  a senior rank you get good postings and  promotion to support the bastards but no  parenting and support from the senior  rank  reads one multiple memes and gifts two  giggity is a catchphrase from the tv  show family guy used to express  sexual excitement a military police  spokesperson tells global news they're  aware of the comments and the matter is  currently under review  survivors of sexual misconduct in the  canadian forces and  others question whether military police  should be trusted to handle sexual  misconduct cases  let alone one involving the country's  former top soldier  they don't have the independence of a  professional police force a civilian  police force  stu kellock served 40 years with the  canadian forces reserve  including 30 with the military police he  says every decision they make  is influenced by the chain of command  these are very dedicated  hard-working and committed individuals  who tried to do the right thing  but unfortunately their chain of command  often comes as an obstruction to that  it's heartbreaking policing expert  christian luprecht says the facebook  comments re-victimize those involved  it's exactly the sort of discourse that  civilian police forces have fought  really hard to root out from their  organizations  he says change has to come from the top  starting with defense minister harjud  sajin  we need the ministers to step in and  draw some clear red lines  a government source told global news  military police have not found any  currently serving military police  officers among those who posted the  comments  abigail beeman global news ottawa  he kept a watchful eye while his  colleagues made one giant leap for  mankind  next remembering the forgotten astronaut  a former astronaut who has been  described as the loneliest man in  history has died  michael collins stayed alone in the  command module during the historic 1969  mission to the moon while  neil armstrong and buzz aldrin went on  the first moonwalk  he said later the earth looked like a  fragile little thing you could obscure  with your fingernail the others got all  the glory but collins was critical to  that mission and the tributes to him  today make that clear  david aiken reports it's one small step  for man while neil armstrong was taking  that giant leap for mankind  michael collins then 38 years old was by  himself  in the command module flying circles  around the dark side of the moon  collins armstrong and buzz aldrin were  the three-man crew in the most famous  flight in history  the one that carried human beings to the  surface of the moon  for the first time and for most of that  mission collins was the pilot  basically he was in the mother ship  making sure everything worked  an absolutely vital role he was the  keystone of that mission  yes indeed they got the flag up now  collins never griped about missing his  chance to walk on the moon  or the fame and glory that came to  armstrong and aldrin  the thing i remember most is the view of  planet earth from a great distance  tiny very shiny blue and white  bright beautiful serene  and fragile mike was wise enough to  see that his place in history was very  important but he wasn't going to go  along with some of the ridiculous  celebrity that can come with that  he was the perfect person to be on that  first mission collins will also be  remembered for his 1974 autobiography  carrying the fire widely considered the  best account of space flight  ever penned by one who flew people have  this curiosity they want to know about  the universe they want to know more  about how  it works how it affects their lives  collins may not have walked on the moon  but he was the one who flew humanity  there first and brought its first  explorers  home safely  and that is global national for this  wednesday buzz aldrin is now the only  surviving crew member of that apollo 11  mission and today  he tweeted this dear mike wherever you  have been or will be you will always  have the fire to carry us deftly  to new heights and to the future we will  miss you  may you rest in peace i'm donna friesen  thanks for watching  hope to see you here again tomorrow take  care of yourselves  [Music]  [Applause]  bye-bye

